  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 AM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nBroad longwave troughing aloft is set up over much of the north \ncentral CONUS and the trof axis will slowly shift eastward today to \nbe positioned through the Mississippi Valley region. At the sfc a \ncold front will be situated northeast to southwest through north \ncentral IL, central MO, and southeast KS this afternoon and evening \nwhich is where it will largely stall out. After one more day of dry \nand mild conditions today, a ripple in the flow aloft and the \nproximity of the front will spur on a few showers an storms by \nThursday morning across parts of SEMO and western portions of \nsouthern IL. After a mainly dry afternoon Thursday, 30-60% PoPs come \nback overnight and through Friday then taper off Saturday morning \nover west KY. Not everyone will see rain during this time frame \nthough. Thursday in particular, a few strong to severe storms \ncan't be ruled out. \n\nHigh pressure over the Four Corners region will expand eastward on \nSunday bringing back highs in the lower 90s and dewpoints in the mid \nto upper 70s. This will push heat indices back into the 100-105 \ndegree range through early week. Dry conditions are expected until \nTuesday evening through Wednesday when 20-30% PoPs return to the \nforecast and bring some potential relief from the heat.",
